If your reference genome lacks a Y chromosome, then reads originating from the biological Y chromosome in your male samples will behave in several different ways during mapping.
This is a very important concept in population genomics.

What happens during mapping?
When you map reads, the aligner (like BWA or Bowtie 2) tries to find the best matching location in the reference genome.

If the reference genome has: autosomes, X chromosome, but NO Y chromosome, then true Y-derived reads have nowhere perfect to align.

Possible outcomes for Y reads
1. Many Y reads fail to map
This is the most common outcome. Because there is no matching sequence in the reference:
Y read → no suitable alignment → unmapped
These reads appear in the BAM file as unmapped reads.
Example SAM flag: 4 = unmapped

2. Some Y reads map incorrectly to similar regions
The mammalian X and Y chromosomes share homologous regions.
So some Y reads may incorrectly map to:
X chromosome
repetitive regions
paralogous autosomal regions

Example:
Y read → partial similarity to X → mapped to X

This can create: false SNPs, inflated heterozygosity, mapping artifacts

3. Highly repetitive Y reads get discarded
The Y chromosome is full of repeats and duplicated regions.

Many aligners: assign low mapping quality or discard multi-mapping reads
So lots of Y reads never contribute to variant calling.
In ddRAD specifically, this effect is usually MUCH smaller than in whole-genome sequencing.
Why?
ddRAD captures only fragments near restriction enzyme sites.
That means: only a tiny fraction of Y chromosome is sequenced and many Y repetitive regions are never sampled

So in practice:
most Y reads are absent or filtered automatically during mapping/variant calling
What this means for your analysis: Since your reference lacks Y chromosome: Good news

Most Y reads likely: failed to map or were filtered out already
So your VCF probably contains very little Y information.

Remaining concern
Some Y-derived reads may have incorrectly mapped to X chromosome or autosomes. That is one reason why removing the X chromosome afterward is a good idea.








































































